<onlyinclude>'''Semiconductor foundry''' is a contract manufacturer that fabricates semiconductor devices on wafers from integrated-circuit designs supplied by customers. A ''pure-play'' foundry concentrates on manufacturing customer designs rather than marketing competing chip products.<ref name="tsmc">[https://www.tsmc.com/english/aboutTSMC TSMC, About TSMC], accessed 2026-11-21.</ref></onlyinclude>

== Role in the semiconductor supply chain ==
A fabless company develops a chip design and contracts wafer fabrication to a foundry. A foundry supplies qualified manufacturing processes, design rules and supporting design infrastructure, while packaging and testing may be performed by the foundry or specialist providers. An integrated device manufacturer (IDM) instead combines design and fabrication within one company, although it may also outsource some wafers.

== Design-to-manufacture interface ==
Foundry process design kits (PDKs) and associated design-rule manuals, device models, standard-cell libraries and qualified electronic design automation flows enable designers to target a particular process. Tape-out transfers a manufacturing-ready layout for mask preparation and wafer processing. Physical design sign-off reduces risk but does not guarantee working silicon.<ref name="oip">[https://www.tsmc.com/english/blog-article-20211025 TSMC, Open Innovation Platform], 2021.</ref>

== Operations and economics ==
Capacity, wafer pricing, process qualification, cycle time, die area and yield influence cost and delivery. High utilization can spread fixed capital costs but also constrains customer scheduling. Yield and performance vary with product, process maturity and operating conditions.

